1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,906
50 miles off the coast of Alabama, a 90-foot fishing

2
00:00:04,906 --> 00:00:06,107
boat is on fire.

3
00:00:06,107 --> 00:00:07,835
Four people are aboard.

4
00:00:08,285 --> 00:00:10,244
Out here, there is no fire truck.

5
00:00:10,244 --> 00:00:11,549
There is no ambulance.

6
00:00:11,549 --> 00:00:12,558
There is no road.

7
00:00:13,008 --> 00:00:15,709
What comes is a Coast Guard airplane, and then a

8
00:00:15,709 --> 00:00:17,003
Coast Guard helicopter.

9
00:00:17,453 --> 00:00:20,197
That same autumn, Coast Guard helicopter crews

10
00:00:20,197 --> 00:00:23,298
lifted people off an oil tanker, two cruise ships, a

11
00:00:23,298 --> 00:00:26,400
container ship, and a rocky shore in Alaska, where a

12
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,786
boater was stranded after his boat sank.

13
00:00:29,236 --> 00:00:32,651
No ambulance can drive to a ship 100 miles out at sea.

14
00:00:32,651 --> 00:00:36,256
So the Coast Guard lowers help down a cable from the sky.

15
00:00:36,706 --> 00:00:39,362
This is how they do it, why it took a war and a

16
00:00:39,362 --> 00:00:42,299
shipwreck to get here, and what happened to the four

17
00:00:42,299 --> 00:00:43,881
people on that burning boat.

18
00:00:44,281 --> 00:00:45,793
No ambulance at sea.

19
00:00:48,193 --> 00:00:51,179
Start with a weekend in November, off Puerto Rico,

20
00:00:51,179 --> 00:00:52,971
and a call to the Coast Guard.

21
00:00:53,971 --> 00:00:57,642
The Daibelli is a small oil tanker, 328 feet long,

22
00:00:57,642 --> 00:00:58,963
flagged in Cyprus.

23
00:00:58,963 --> 00:01:02,340
That weekend she was at anchor off Vieques, an

24
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:04,689
island just east of Puerto Rico.

25
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:08,947
She was built in 2022, and her inspection record is clean.

26
00:01:09,397 --> 00:01:11,983
On Saturday night, the ship's master called the

27
00:01:11,983 --> 00:01:14,183
Coast Guard's rescue center in San Juan.

28
00:01:14,183 --> 00:01:15,669
Four of his crew were sick.

29
00:01:15,669 --> 00:01:17,814
The suspected cause was food poisoning.

30
00:01:18,264 --> 00:01:20,527
A ship at anchor has no emergency room.

31
00:01:20,527 --> 00:01:23,312
The hospital they needed was on the main island,

32
00:01:23,312 --> 00:01:24,298
across the water.

33
00:01:24,298 --> 00:01:27,142
The Coast Guard decided that a helicopter was the

34
00:01:27,142 --> 00:01:29,404
best way to get the four of them there.

35
00:01:29,854 --> 00:01:33,045
On Sunday morning, an MH-60T Jayhawk from Coast

36
00:01:33,045 --> 00:01:36,235
Guard Air Station Borinquen reached the tanker.

37
00:01:36,685 --> 00:01:40,426
A Jayhawk usually flies with a crew of four. Two pilots.

38
00:01:40,426 --> 00:01:41,628
A flight mechanic.

39
00:01:41,628 --> 00:01:43,031
And a rescue swimmer.

40
00:01:43,481 --> 00:01:45,080
Then came the first problem.

41
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,765
The crew had trouble finding a safe spot on the

42
00:01:47,765 --> 00:01:49,650
tanker's deck to lower the hoist.

43
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,165
Lieutenant Stan Raymond, the co-pilot, said the ship

44
00:01:53,165 --> 00:01:55,817
presented a challenging hoisting environment.

45
00:01:55,817 --> 00:01:58,705
The crew worked out what he called the safest and

46
00:01:58,705 --> 00:02:00,120
most effective location.

47
00:02:00,570 --> 00:02:02,162
Then the hoist went to work.

48
00:02:02,162 --> 00:02:05,118
The camera on the helicopter shows the rescue basket

49
00:02:05,118 --> 00:02:07,903
going down to the deck, and coming back up, again

50
00:02:07,903 --> 00:02:08,472
and again.

51
00:02:08,922 --> 00:02:12,090
All four mariners were hoisted into the helicopter.

52
00:02:12,090 --> 00:02:15,258
The crew flew them to San Juan's main airport, Luis

53
00:02:15,258 --> 00:02:16,003
Muñoz Marín.

54
00:02:16,003 --> 00:02:19,544
Medical crews met them there and took them to a hospital.

55
00:02:19,994 --> 00:02:23,242
I'm grateful, Raymond said, that our crew was able

56
00:02:23,242 --> 00:02:26,620
to safely evacuate all four mariners and get them to

57
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:28,634
a higher level of medical care.

58
00:02:29,084 --> 00:02:31,134
Notice one detail in the reports.

59
00:02:31,134 --> 00:02:34,054
At the airport, Coast Guard Sector San Juan had

60
00:02:34,054 --> 00:02:36,477
arranged two things for the helicopter:

61
00:02:36,477 --> 00:02:39,645
medical services for the patients, and fuel for the

62
00:02:39,645 --> 00:02:40,204
aircraft.

63
00:02:40,204 --> 00:02:42,130
The patients needed a hospital.

64
00:02:42,130 --> 00:02:43,807
The helicopter needed fuel.

65
00:02:44,257 --> 00:02:47,360
By Monday, the Daibelli was under way again, headed

66
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,089
for the U.S.

67
00:02:48,089 --> 00:02:51,009
Virgin Islands, back on her usual run around the

68
00:02:51,009 --> 00:02:51,617
Caribbean.

69
00:02:52,067 --> 00:02:54,466
Customs and Border Protection, the U.S.

70
00:02:54,466 --> 00:02:57,479
Public Health Service and Puerto Rico's emergency

71
00:02:57,479 --> 00:02:59,447
medical services all had a part.

72
00:02:59,447 --> 00:03:02,645
The Coast Guard does not say how the four are doing.

73
00:03:02,645 --> 00:03:04,306
Their privacy stays theirs.

74
00:03:04,306 --> 00:03:06,212
What it does show is the hoist.

75
00:03:06,812 --> 00:03:09,374
For most of history, none of this was possible.

76
00:03:10,374 --> 00:03:13,439
A sick sailor far from shore waited for port, or for

77
00:03:13,439 --> 00:03:14,206
another ship.

78
00:03:14,206 --> 00:03:17,212
Then, in the middle of a world war, came a disaster

79
00:03:17,212 --> 00:03:19,039
at the door of New York Harbor.

80
00:03:19,489 --> 00:03:22,620
On the morning of January 3, 1944, the Navy

81
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:25,969
destroyer USS Turner was anchored near Ambrose

82
00:03:25,969 --> 00:03:27,862
Light, outside the harbor.

83
00:03:28,312 --> 00:03:31,328
Explosions tore her apart, and fires set off her

84
00:03:31,328 --> 00:03:32,019
ammunition.

85
00:03:32,019 --> 00:03:35,160
According to the Coast Guard's historian, a second

86
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:38,176
explosion, 47 minutes after the first, sank her.

87
00:03:38,626 --> 00:03:41,790
Survivors were taken to a hospital at Sandy Hook,

88
00:03:41,790 --> 00:03:42,500
New Jersey.

89
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:45,147
The wounded urgently needed blood plasma.

90
00:03:45,597 --> 00:03:47,242
But a nor'easter was raging.

91
00:03:47,242 --> 00:03:49,121
High winds, snow squalls, sleet.

92
00:03:49,121 --> 00:03:50,413
Airfields were closed.

93
00:03:50,413 --> 00:03:52,057
Every aircraft was grounded.

94
00:03:52,507 --> 00:03:54,330
Except one kind of aircraft.

95
00:03:54,330 --> 00:03:57,519
At Floyd Bennett Field in Brooklyn, a Coast Guard

96
00:03:57,519 --> 00:04:00,904
pilot named Frank Erickson, an early champion of the

97
00:04:00,904 --> 00:04:03,052
helicopter, had a Sikorsky HNS-1.

98
00:04:03,502 --> 00:04:05,819
Erickson was not a typical aviator.

99
00:04:05,819 --> 00:04:09,262
Born near Portland, Oregon, he enlisted in the Navy,

100
00:04:09,262 --> 00:04:12,638
became a midshipman, resigned, and then enlisted in

101
00:04:12,638 --> 00:04:13,697
the Coast Guard.

102
00:04:13,697 --> 00:04:16,941
He graduated from the Coast Guard Academy and was

103
00:04:16,941 --> 00:04:18,331
commissioned in 1931.

104
00:04:18,781 --> 00:04:21,803
He served on cutters, and he was in Honolulu when

105
00:04:21,803 --> 00:04:23,530
Japan attacked Pearl Harbor.

106
00:04:23,530 --> 00:04:25,812
After that, he pushed for a new idea.

107
00:04:25,812 --> 00:04:28,525
He saw the helicopter as a search and rescue

108
00:04:28,525 --> 00:04:31,671
machine, and also as protection for convoys against

109
00:04:31,671 --> 00:04:32,349
submarines.

110
00:04:32,799 --> 00:04:35,932
Around ten in the morning, an admiral asked whether

111
00:04:35,932 --> 00:04:38,634
a helicopter could fly plasma to Sandy Hook.

112
00:04:38,634 --> 00:04:40,231
Erickson said yes at once.

113
00:04:40,231 --> 00:04:42,934
He had never flown in conditions like these.

114
00:04:42,934 --> 00:04:45,637
Floyd Bennett Field itself was closed by the

115
00:04:45,637 --> 00:04:48,278
weather, so permission had to come from the

116
00:04:48,278 --> 00:04:50,550
commander of the New York Air Patrol.

117
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,983
At 10:20, with Ensign Walter Bolton beside him,

118
00:04:53,983 --> 00:04:57,537
Erickson lifted off into swirling snow and gusting wind.

119
00:04:57,537 --> 00:05:00,773
Visibility was so low that, he said later, they had

120
00:05:00,773 --> 00:05:03,692
to feel their way around the ships in the bay.

121
00:05:04,142 --> 00:05:06,896
At Battery Park, on the tip of Manhattan, Bolton

122
00:05:06,896 --> 00:05:09,821
stayed behind while two cases of plasma were lashed

123
00:05:09,821 --> 00:05:11,370
to the helicopter's floats.

124
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:14,543
Trees blocked the way ahead, so he backed the

125
00:05:14,543 --> 00:05:17,688
helicopter out, and at about 10:55, it rose straight

126
00:05:17,688 --> 00:05:18,777
up into the storm.

127
00:05:19,227 --> 00:05:23,166
At 11:09, Erickson landed at the Coast Guard station

128
00:05:23,166 --> 00:05:24,226
at Sandy Hook.

129
00:05:24,226 --> 00:05:25,514
About 14 minutes.

130
00:05:25,514 --> 00:05:27,105
The plasma was there.

131
00:05:27,555 --> 00:05:30,414
Erickson said the weather was such that this flight

132
00:05:30,414 --> 00:05:33,498
could not have been made in any other type of aircraft.

133
00:05:33,948 --> 00:05:36,991
The Navy's historians call it the first helicopter

134
00:05:36,991 --> 00:05:38,270
lifesaving operation.

135
00:05:38,270 --> 00:05:39,609
It carried no patient.

136
00:05:39,609 --> 00:05:40,704
It carried plasma.

137
00:05:40,704 --> 00:05:43,748
Erickson went on to pioneer some of the techniques

138
00:05:43,748 --> 00:05:45,757
the Navy and Coast Guard adapted.

139
00:05:45,757 --> 00:05:48,557
And it proved that a helicopter could go where

140
00:05:48,557 --> 00:05:49,957
nothing else could fly.

141
00:05:50,407 --> 00:05:52,265
The next step was the cable.

142
00:05:52,265 --> 00:05:55,516
Late in November 1945, a storm drove a Texaco oil

143
00:05:55,516 --> 00:05:59,033
barge onto Penfield Reef, off Fairfield, Connecticut.

144
00:05:59,033 --> 00:06:00,361
Waves broke over it.

145
00:06:00,361 --> 00:06:01,688
It was coming apart.

146
00:06:01,688 --> 00:06:05,072
Two men were trapped aboard, and rescue boats could

147
00:06:05,072 --> 00:06:06,067
not reach them.

148
00:06:06,517 --> 00:06:09,448
Sikorsky's chief test pilot, Jimmy Viner, and an

149
00:06:09,448 --> 00:06:12,562
Army Air Forces pilot, Jackson Beighle, flew out in

150
00:06:12,562 --> 00:06:15,676
an R-5 helicopter fitted with a hoist and a lifting

151
00:06:15,676 --> 00:06:16,164
harness.

152
00:06:16,164 --> 00:06:19,278
Viner had only tested that harness a few feet above

153
00:06:19,278 --> 00:06:19,950
the ground.

154
00:06:19,950 --> 00:06:22,453
The winds were close to 60 miles an hour.

155
00:06:22,903 --> 00:06:25,164
They lifted the men off one at a time.

156
00:06:25,164 --> 00:06:27,961
The Vertical Flight Society has made the spot a

157
00:06:27,961 --> 00:06:30,877
heritage site, for what is widely regarded as the

158
00:06:30,877 --> 00:06:33,733
world's first successful helicopter hoist rescue

159
00:06:33,733 --> 00:06:34,388
over water.

160
00:06:34,388 --> 00:06:37,422
It showed that a helicopter could pull people off a

161
00:06:37,422 --> 00:06:38,434
wreck on a cable.

162
00:06:39,034 --> 00:06:42,950
82 years later, every rescue flight still runs on a clock:

163
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:44,773
distance, fuel and weather.

164
00:06:45,773 --> 00:06:49,128
The helicopter that flew to the tanker, the MH-60T

165
00:06:49,128 --> 00:06:52,283
Jayhawk, is what the Coast Guard calls a medium

166
00:06:52,283 --> 00:06:54,027
range recovery helicopter.

167
00:06:54,027 --> 00:06:56,980
Its hoist raises and lowers rescue swimmers,

168
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:58,591
survivors and equipment.

169
00:06:59,041 --> 00:07:02,664
The Coast Guard's own fact sheet gives three numbers.

170
00:07:02,664 --> 00:07:05,124
A range of about 700 nautical miles.

171
00:07:05,124 --> 00:07:06,833
A top speed of 170 knots.

172
00:07:06,833 --> 00:07:08,542
And 6.5 hours in the air.

173
00:07:08,992 --> 00:07:10,549
Those numbers are the clock.

174
00:07:10,549 --> 00:07:12,217
Every mile out is a mile back.

175
00:07:12,217 --> 00:07:15,053
Every minute spent hovering over a ship is fuel the

176
00:07:15,053 --> 00:07:16,554
crew will need to get home.

177
00:07:17,004 --> 00:07:20,559
In September, a Jayhawk crew from Borinquen flew to

178
00:07:20,559 --> 00:07:24,323
a commercial ship about 100 nautical miles east of St.

179
00:07:24,323 --> 00:07:24,880
Maarten.

180
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:28,156
The Coast Guard called it a complex, long-range

181
00:07:28,156 --> 00:07:29,480
medical evacuation.

182
00:07:29,930 --> 00:07:32,460
Infrared video shows the ship's deck.

183
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:35,946
The crew hoisted a 46-year-old mariner and flew him

184
00:07:35,946 --> 00:07:39,363
to the Centro Médico trauma center in San Juan for

185
00:07:39,363 --> 00:07:41,209
emergency specialized care.

186
00:07:41,659 --> 00:07:45,322
On Halloween, off San Francisco, the job went to

187
00:07:45,322 --> 00:07:49,061
another Coast Guard helicopter, an MH-65 Dolphin.

188
00:07:49,061 --> 00:07:52,723
The patient was a 53-year-old man on a container

189
00:07:52,723 --> 00:07:54,173
ship, 34 miles out.

190
00:07:54,623 --> 00:07:58,042
Overhead, a C-27 Spartan airplane from Air Station

191
00:07:58,042 --> 00:08:01,598
Sacramento provided coordination and overwatch while

192
00:08:01,598 --> 00:08:03,444
the Dolphin made the hoist.

193
00:08:03,894 --> 00:08:05,443
Often, that is the pattern.

194
00:08:05,443 --> 00:08:08,541
One aircraft to search, guide and watch. One to hoist.

195
00:08:08,541 --> 00:08:11,810
And on the end of the cable, a human being from the crew.

196
00:08:12,410 --> 00:08:15,092
For decades, that person on the cable was missing.

197
00:08:16,092 --> 00:08:16,779
The S.S.

198
00:08:16,779 --> 00:08:20,897
Marine Electric was a 605-foot cargo ship with a

199
00:08:20,897 --> 00:08:21,841
crew of 34.

200
00:08:21,841 --> 00:08:25,273
On February 12, 1983, about 30 miles off

201
00:08:25,273 --> 00:08:29,221
Chincoteague, Virginia, she capsized and sank.

202
00:08:29,671 --> 00:08:33,735
Her first distress call went out at 2:51 in the morning.

203
00:08:33,735 --> 00:08:35,550
The water was 37 degrees.

204
00:08:35,999 --> 00:08:37,760
Coast Guard helicopters came.

205
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:40,673
One crew lifted a survivor from a life raft in a

206
00:08:40,673 --> 00:08:44,011
rescue basket, and pulled another man from a life ring.

207
00:08:44,011 --> 00:08:47,046
A Norwegian merchant ship spotted a third man in a

208
00:08:47,046 --> 00:08:49,595
lifeboat, and a helicopter lifted him out.

209
00:08:50,045 --> 00:08:53,328
But in 1983, the Coast Guard did not have any rescue

210
00:08:53,328 --> 00:08:53,896
swimmers.

211
00:08:53,896 --> 00:08:56,926
No one on its helicopters was trained to go down

212
00:08:56,926 --> 00:09:00,146
into that water and bring a survivor to the basket.

213
00:09:00,596 --> 00:09:01,488
The Navy did.

214
00:09:01,488 --> 00:09:04,988
Petty Officer 2nd Class James McCann, a Navy rescue

215
00:09:04,988 --> 00:09:08,556
swimmer from Naval Air Station Oceana, went into the

216
00:09:08,556 --> 00:09:08,968
water.

217
00:09:09,418 --> 00:09:12,341
The Coast Guard's own account says one rescue

218
00:09:12,341 --> 00:09:15,588
swimmer recovered five unresponsive sailors before

219
00:09:15,588 --> 00:09:17,992
hypothermia left him unable to go on.

220
00:09:18,442 --> 00:09:21,644
Of 34 men, 3 survived. 31 died.

221
00:09:21,644 --> 00:09:24,640
Rescuers recovered 24 bodies.

222
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:27,016
7 men were never found.

223
00:09:27,466 --> 00:09:30,616
A board of inquiry later found that inspectors had

224
00:09:30,616 --> 00:09:34,143
missed problems on the ship, despite recent inspections.

225
00:09:34,143 --> 00:09:37,356
It concluded the sinking most likely came from poor

226
00:09:37,356 --> 00:09:40,317
cargo hatches and deck plating, which let waves

227
00:09:40,317 --> 00:09:41,892
flood the forward spaces.

228
00:09:42,342 --> 00:09:45,596
The investigation that followed changed ship safety.

229
00:09:45,596 --> 00:09:48,726
It led to new rules for cold water survival suits,

230
00:09:48,726 --> 00:09:52,231
enclosed lifeboats and tighter inspections of old ships.

231
00:09:52,231 --> 00:09:55,673
70 vessels from the Second World War era were scrapped.

232
00:09:56,123 --> 00:09:58,111
And it changed the helicopters.

233
00:09:58,111 --> 00:10:01,383
With funding from the Coast Guard Authorization Act

234
00:10:01,383 --> 00:10:04,397
of 1984, the Coast Guard started its own rescue

235
00:10:04,397 --> 00:10:05,424
swimmer program.

236
00:10:05,874 --> 00:10:08,526
This is what that program looks like today.

237
00:10:08,526 --> 00:10:11,671
On November 5, in training near Mona Island, Puerto

238
00:10:11,671 --> 00:10:14,693
Rico, a rescue swimmer from Air Station Borinquen

239
00:10:14,693 --> 00:10:17,715
rode the hoist from a Jayhawk all the way down to

240
00:10:17,715 --> 00:10:19,812
the sea, then back up to the door.

241
00:10:20,262 --> 00:10:24,174
In March 1985, the first rescue swimmers reported to

242
00:10:24,174 --> 00:10:27,409
Air Station Elizabeth City, North Carolina.

243
00:10:27,409 --> 00:10:31,245
Two months later, the program saved its first life.

244
00:10:31,245 --> 00:10:35,308
By 1991, rescue swimmers were at 23 Coast Guard bases.

245
00:10:35,908 --> 00:10:38,904
41 years later, a crew from that same air station

246
00:10:38,904 --> 00:10:40,739
got a call from a cruise ship.

247
00:10:41,739 --> 00:10:44,667
Norwegian Bliss carries about 4,000 guests.

248
00:10:44,667 --> 00:10:47,935
On November 2, she was about 32 miles off Oregon

249
00:10:47,935 --> 00:10:51,407
Inlet, North Carolina, on a cruise from New York to

250
00:10:51,407 --> 00:10:52,224
the Bahamas.

251
00:10:52,224 --> 00:10:55,560
The ship reported that a passenger needed medical

252
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:56,241
attention.

253
00:10:56,691 --> 00:10:59,739
The ship had left New York the day before, on an

254
00:10:59,739 --> 00:11:01,199
eight-night round trip.

255
00:11:01,199 --> 00:11:02,786
November 2 was a sea day.

256
00:11:02,786 --> 00:11:06,468
No hospital, no port, only open water off the Outer Banks.

257
00:11:06,918 --> 00:11:09,807
An aircrew from Air Station Elizabeth City flew out.

258
00:11:09,807 --> 00:11:12,084
This is the view from a camera inside the

259
00:11:12,084 --> 00:11:13,084
helicopter's door.

260
00:11:13,534 --> 00:11:16,169
Inside, the crew wear helmets marked USCG.

261
00:11:16,169 --> 00:11:17,235
The door is open.

262
00:11:17,235 --> 00:11:20,497
Below them is the top deck of a cruise ship, and the

263
00:11:20,497 --> 00:11:21,375
sea beyond it.

264
00:11:21,825 --> 00:11:24,640
A crew member rides the hoist down to the ship's deck.

265
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:27,610
The yellow circle below is the spot marked for the winch.

266
00:11:28,060 --> 00:11:30,965
Then the patient comes up, strapped into a rescue

267
00:11:30,965 --> 00:11:33,989
litter, turning slowly on the cable, until the crew

268
00:11:33,989 --> 00:11:35,886
can pull it in through the door.

269
00:11:36,336 --> 00:11:39,364
The Coast Guard hoisted an onboard nurse as well.

270
00:11:39,364 --> 00:11:42,330
The aircrew flew both of them to Sentara Norfolk

271
00:11:42,330 --> 00:11:44,679
General Hospital in Norfolk, Virginia.

272
00:11:45,129 --> 00:11:47,877
Officials said the passenger was transferred in

273
00:11:47,877 --> 00:11:48,871
stable condition.

274
00:11:48,871 --> 00:11:51,035
No change to the cruise was reported.

275
00:11:51,485 --> 00:11:54,733
In September, a Borinquen crew did the same job in

276
00:11:54,733 --> 00:11:58,111
the dark, over the helicopter pad of the cruise ship

277
00:11:58,111 --> 00:12:00,450
Allure of the Seas, off Puerto Rico.

278
00:12:00,450 --> 00:12:03,763
The patient, a crew member, was taken to a hospital

279
00:12:03,763 --> 00:12:04,608
in Aguadilla.

280
00:12:05,208 --> 00:12:06,732
Now, back to the burning boat.

281
00:12:07,732 --> 00:12:11,130
On October 13, a 90-foot fishing vessel caught fire

282
00:12:11,130 --> 00:12:13,996
about 50 miles off Dauphin Island, Alabama.

283
00:12:13,996 --> 00:12:15,595
Four people were aboard.

284
00:12:15,595 --> 00:12:18,927
This video, released by the Coast Guard, shows the

285
00:12:18,927 --> 00:12:19,860
fire up close.

286
00:12:20,310 --> 00:12:23,803
The first Coast Guard aircraft to find it was not a

287
00:12:23,803 --> 00:12:24,557
helicopter.

288
00:12:24,557 --> 00:12:27,777
It was an HC-144 Ocean Sentry airplane from the

289
00:12:27,777 --> 00:12:31,134
Coast Guard's Aviation Training Center in Mobile.

290
00:12:31,584 --> 00:12:35,459
The Ocean Sentry is built for exactly this kind of search.

291
00:12:35,459 --> 00:12:38,599
The Coast Guard's fact sheet lists more than 10

292
00:12:38,599 --> 00:12:42,074
hours of endurance, a range of 2,100 nautical miles,

293
00:12:42,074 --> 00:12:44,680
and search radar with infrared sensors.

294
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:48,422
It can stay up long after a helicopter has to turn home.

295
00:12:48,872 --> 00:12:51,851
On infrared, the fire shows up as a blaze of white

296
00:12:51,851 --> 00:12:53,042
heat on a black sea.

297
00:12:53,042 --> 00:12:56,140
The plane found the boat, then guided a Jayhawk from

298
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:57,928
Air Station New Orleans to it.

299
00:12:58,378 --> 00:13:00,782
The boat kept burning while the helicopter worked.

300
00:13:01,232 --> 00:13:04,371
The Jayhawk crew rescued all four people and flew

301
00:13:04,371 --> 00:13:06,741
them to the Aviation Training Center.

302
00:13:06,741 --> 00:13:08,920
Officials said no one was injured.

303
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:11,226
The cause of the fire was not known.

304
00:13:11,676 --> 00:13:12,638
Four people.

305
00:13:12,638 --> 00:13:14,241
50 miles from shore.

306
00:13:14,241 --> 00:13:15,444
A boat on fire.

307
00:13:15,444 --> 00:13:18,892
And a crew that came down out of the night.

308
00:13:19,492 --> 00:13:21,007
These were not the only calls.

309
00:13:22,007 --> 00:13:24,579
Off Alaska in September, near Murder Cove on

310
00:13:24,579 --> 00:13:27,442
Admiralty Island, a Jayhawk crew from Air Station

311
00:13:27,442 --> 00:13:30,422
Sitka found a boater whose fishing vessel had sunk.

312
00:13:30,422 --> 00:13:32,760
His life raft had begun taking on water.

313
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:35,740
The crew lowered the hoist to the rocks and brought

314
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:36,149
him up.

315
00:13:36,599 --> 00:13:39,587
On Lake Michigan in November, near Green Island,

316
00:13:39,587 --> 00:13:42,575
Wisconsin, a crew from Air Station Traverse City

317
00:13:42,575 --> 00:13:45,625
hoisted a man from a sailboat that had lost power

318
00:13:45,625 --> 00:13:46,621
and run aground.

319
00:13:46,621 --> 00:13:49,671
A Coast Guard response boat from Station Sturgeon

320
00:13:49,671 --> 00:13:52,535
Bay also came, but shallow water kept it back.

321
00:13:52,985 --> 00:13:56,461
In South Carolina, a crew from Air Station Savannah

322
00:13:56,461 --> 00:14:00,005
hoisted six people from a grounded 18-foot boat near

323
00:14:00,005 --> 00:14:03,277
Turtle Island, at night, and flew them to Hunter

324
00:14:03,277 --> 00:14:04,231
Army Airfield.

325
00:14:04,231 --> 00:14:05,322
No one was hurt.

326
00:14:05,322 --> 00:14:08,389
Off Hawaii, a Dolphin crew from Barbers Point

327
00:14:08,389 --> 00:14:11,593
medevaced a man with a medical condition from a

328
00:14:11,593 --> 00:14:14,933
cargo ship 86 miles out, and flew him to Hilo for

329
00:14:14,933 --> 00:14:16,296
higher medical care.

330
00:14:16,746 --> 00:14:19,460
Put the cases from this story together and you get

331
00:14:19,460 --> 00:14:21,306
the Coast Guard's ordinary autumn.

332
00:14:21,756 --> 00:14:25,717
In 1944, one helicopter carried two cases of plasma

333
00:14:25,717 --> 00:14:28,436
for 14 minutes, and it was history.

334
00:14:28,886 --> 00:14:32,426
In 1983, Coast Guard helicopters reached the men in

335
00:14:32,426 --> 00:14:36,037
the water, and the Coast Guard had no one of its own

336
00:14:36,037 --> 00:14:37,564
to send in after them.

337
00:14:38,014 --> 00:14:42,425
And in big disasters, the system Erickson started

338
00:14:42,425 --> 00:14:43,326
scales up.

339
00:14:43,326 --> 00:14:47,377
The Coast Guard's historian counts 12,535 air

340
00:14:47,377 --> 00:14:51,879
rescues in two weeks during Hurricane Katrina, and

341
00:14:51,879 --> 00:14:56,201
11,363 lives saved in the 2017 hurricane season.

342
00:14:56,651 --> 00:14:59,774
Today, a crew of four flies out 100 miles, lowers

343
00:14:59,774 --> 00:15:03,089
one of its own onto a ship's deck, and brings people

344
00:15:03,089 --> 00:15:04,300
back to a hospital.

345
00:15:04,750 --> 00:15:07,115
No ambulance can reach them out there.

346
00:15:07,115 --> 00:15:09,730
So the Coast Guard sends one down a cable.

347
00:15:10,330 --> 00:15:11,867
Next time on KEPT ALIVE:

348
00:15:11,867 --> 00:15:14,750
another real save, and the history behind it.

349
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:16,340
For education only.

350
00:15:16,340 --> 00:15:17,961
This is not medical advice.

351
00:15:17,961 --> 00:15:20,783
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
